            </p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">
                How to Count Words Across All Tabs in Google Docs (2026 Guide)
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
                Google Docs tabs are great for organizing long documents — until
                you need a total word count. The built-in counter only counts
                the tab you're on. Here are three ways to get the full count,
                including the method I use for my own book projects.
            </p>
        </header>

        <section class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
                I still remember the panic I felt while writing my first
                multi-tab thesis in Google Docs. I had six tabs, one for each
                chapter, and I needed the total word count for submission. The
                built-in counter said 2,100 words — but that was just chapter
                one. I spent the next hour manually counting each tab with a
                calculator.
            </p>
            <p>
                There has to be a better way, I thought. And there is. After
                months of experimenting, I found three reliable methods. This
                guide covers all of them, from the slowest (but simplest) to the
                fastest (the one I actually use now).
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Quick Answer: How to Count Words Across All Tabs
            </h2>
            <p>
                Google Docs does not have a built-in feature to count words
                across all tabs at once. To get the total, you need to either
                combine all tabs into one document or paste text from each tab
                into an external word counter. The fastest option is an online
                tool — I'll show you exactly how to use it below.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Why Google Docs Doesn't Count All Tabs
            </h2>
            <p>
                Google introduced tabs in Docs in 2024. The feature is designed
                to treat each tab as a separate section, which makes sense for
                organizing long documents. But the word counter was built long
                before tabs existed, and it still works the old way:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Press <strong>Ctrl + Shift + C</strong> (Windows) or <strong
                        >Cmd + Shift + C</strong
                    > (Mac)
                </li>
                <li>
                    The counter shows the count for the <strong
                        >active tab only</strong>
                </li>
                <li>
                    Switching tabs updates the count, but there's no combined
                    total
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Google hasn't announced plans to fix this. So we work around it.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Method 1: Combine All Tabs into One Document (Slower)
            </h2>
            <p>
                This is the most straightforward method, and it's what I used
                for my first few multi-tab projects.
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Open your Google Doc with multiple tabs.</li>
                <li>
                    Click the first tab and press <strong>Ctrl + A</strong> to select
                    all text.
                </li>
                <li>Press <strong>Ctrl + C</strong> to copy.</li>
                <li>Open a new blank Google Doc.</li>
                <li>Press <strong>Ctrl + V</strong> to paste.</li>
                <li>
                    Go back to the original doc and repeat for each tab, pasting
                    each one after the previous.
                </li>
                <li>
                    In the new doc, press <strong>Ctrl + Shift + C</strong> to see
                    the combined word count.
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                <strong>Pros:</strong> Works with Google's native tools, no extensions
                needed.<br />
                <strong>Cons:</strong> Tedious with more than 3–4 tabs, and you end
                up with a duplicate document.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Method 2: Paste into a Free Online Word Counter (Fastest)
            </h2>
            <p>
                This is the method I use now for all my multi-tab work. It takes
                about 30 seconds and gives you more information than Google Docs
                ever could.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Step-by-Step:</h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Open your Google Doc with multiple tabs.</li>
                <li>
                    Open our{" "}
                    <a
                        href="/tools/word-counter"
                        class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                    >
                        free Word Counter tool
                    </a>{" "}
                    in a new browser tab.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Go back to your doc. Select all text in the first tab (<strong
                        >Ctrl + A</strong
                    >), copy (<strong>Ctrl + C</strong>).
                </li>
                <li>Paste it into the Word Counter tool.</li>
                <li>
                    Repeat for each tab — select all, copy, paste at the end of
                    the text already in the tool.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Once all tabs are pasted, the tool shows the total word
                    count, character count, sentence count, and reading time.
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                <strong>Pro tip:</strong> If you have 10+ tabs, do them in batches
                of 5, then paste the batches together. It's easier to track which
                tabs you've already copied.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Why I Prefer This Method
            </h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>No duplicate document</strong> cluttering your Google
                    Drive
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Extra metrics</strong> — character count, sentence count,
                    reading time
                </li>
                <li><strong>Real-time updates</strong> as you paste</li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Works on any device</strong> — laptop, tablet, phone
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Privacy-friendly</strong> — nothing is uploaded to a server
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Method 3: Google Docs Add-ons (For Advanced Use)
            </h2>
            <p>
                There are third-party Google Docs add-ons that offer enhanced
                word count features, including cross-tab counting. These work,
                but I generally avoid them for three reasons:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>They require access to your Google account</li>
                <li>Many are abandoned or poorly maintained</li>
                <li>Some have privacy policies I don't trust</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                If you want to explore them anyway, search "word count" in the
                Google Workspace Marketplace. Just check the permissions before
                installing.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Comparison: Which Method Should You Use?
            </h2>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Method</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Time</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Best For</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
