                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Combine into new doc</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">2–5 min</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">2–3 tabs only</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Online word counter</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">30 sec</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Most cases — fastest and most detailed</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">Google Docs add-on</td>
                            <td class="p-3">Varies</td>
                            <td class="p-3"
                                >Advanced users who want a permanent solution</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Can You Set a Word Count Limit in Google Docs?
            </h2>
            <p>
                This is a question I get asked a lot, and the short answer is: <strong
                    >no, Google Docs has no built-in word limit feature</strong
                >. You can't tell it to warn you when you go over 1,500 words.
            </p>
            <p>
                What you <em>can</em> do:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Enable <strong>"Display word count while typing"</strong> from
                    Tools → Word count. This shows the count in the bottom-left corner
                    as you write.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Set a mental or written target, and check the count
                    periodically using{" "}
                    <a
                        href="/tools/word-counter"
                        class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                    >
                        our Word Counter
                    </a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Use a Google Docs add-on that supports word count warnings —
                    though again, check the permissions.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Common Mistakes to Avoid
            </h2>
            <p>
                After helping several friends and colleagues with this exact
                problem, here are the mistakes I see most often:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Forgetting to select all text in a tab:</strong> If you
                    don't press Ctrl + A first, you'll only copy the visible portion.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Pasting into the wrong spot:</strong> When pasting multiple
                    tabs, paste them at the end, not at the beginning. Otherwise your
                    totals will be off.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Trusting different tools:</strong> Different word counters
                    handle hyphens, contractions, and symbols differently. Pick one
                    tool and use it consistently.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Not checking character count:</strong> Some submissions
                    have character limits, not just word limits. Always check both.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Frequently Asked Questions
            </h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 mt-3">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Does Google Docs have a way to count all tabs at once?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        No. As of 2026, Google Docs does not have a built-in
                        feature to count words across all tabs. You must either
                        combine the tabs or use an external tool.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Is there a keyboard shortcut for total word count?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        No. Ctrl + Shift + C shows the count only for the active
                        tab. There is no shortcut for the combined total.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Will Google add this feature in the future?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Google hasn't announced plans to add cross-tab word
                        counting. Until they do, external tools remain the
                        fastest option.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Can I count words in a specific tab only?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Yes. Click on the tab, then press Ctrl + Shift + C to
                        see that tab's word count. This works for any single
                        tab.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Does copying text count the header and footer?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        No. When you press Ctrl + A, it only selects the main
                        document body — not headers, footers, or footnotes. If
                        those matter for your count, copy them manually.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Final Thoughts</h2>
            <p>
                Google Docs tabs are great for organizing long projects, but
                they come with a word-counting limitation that can waste real
                time. If you're working with just one or two tabs, combining
                them works fine. But for anything bigger — theses, books, long
                reports — using an online word counter is faster, more detailed,
                and less error-prone.
            </p>
